{
	title: 'Socket transport and host takeover',
	description: 'Processes sharing a state root find each other through one Unix socket in it: the first to bind becomes host, later ones connect as clients, and a stale socket left by a dead host is detected and replaced. When the host exits, a surviving client becomes the new host and the others reconnect to it. Messages are line-delimited ASON. The host process runs its own terminal client through the same protocol. Test with several processes on a temp state root. Web clients and cross-machine peers are out of scope, but nothing here should rule them out.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['6']
}
